--TEST--
an unqualified shape written at `Core\Http\Response::jsonAs` is refused where it is written, and the reply's decode site reads exactly as the request's — `rule:security/derived-codec-qualifiers`, `rule:security/tainted-sources`
--FILE--
<?nvs
Core\Test::answerHttp("https://api.example.com/v1/me", 200, {body: "{\"name\":\"ada\"}"});
var $reply = Core\Http\Client::get("https://api.example.com/v1/me");

// `rule:security/tainted-sources` makes a reply's body a peer's octets in the
// same sense a request's is, so the shape written here is the declaration they
// land in. An unqualified text field is a promise nothing is making, and it is
// refused where the shape is written: there is no declaration further down the
// file to carry the qualifier instead.
var $me = $reply->jsonAs<{name: string}>();

// A field carrying no text is not in the diagnosis at all -- a number holds no
// octets a sink could read -- so this line is silent.
var $id = $reply->jsonAs<{id: int}>();

// `tainted {…}` is the answer to the verbosity rather than an opt-out, and it
// distributes to every text-carrying field, so one word answers a shape of two
// and this line is silent as well.
var $ok = $reply->jsonAs<tainted {name: string, note: string}>();
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0810]: `name` is declared `string` in `{name: string}`, and `Core\Http\Response::jsonAs` answers a peer's octets
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
